WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.950 align:start position:0%
 
spider  the book is called an american genocide  the united states in the california  indian catastrophe 1846 to 1873  the author  ucla professor of history benjamin  madley professor madley in your book you  write that between 1846 and 1870  california's native american population  plunged from perhaps a hundred and fifty  thousand to thirty thousand how did that  occur  so there are many things that we  understand already about this story we  know that disease  exposure  starvation  those were all major factors not only in  this population decline but in the  suppression of demographic rebound what  we didn't know in so much detail and  with so much clarity before this book  was all of the other factors and how  they played in  systematic unfree labor regimes  kidnapping  hundreds of homicides and over 370  separate massacres carried out by  vigilantes  volunteer state militiamen and elements  of the united states army all of which  come together to provide a very  convincing damning if you will argument  that what took place in california  between 1846 and 1873  was in fact a case of genocide according  to the 1948 u.n genocide convention why  1846  1846 is the year that the united states  invaded california and took california  from mexico as part of the  mexican-american war and so the story  begins there but it is also the year in  which the very first major massacre of  california indian people in this case  yana or wintu people in 1846 took place  at the hands of elements of the united  states army under john c fremont a  massacre of by all accounts peaceable  california indian people who were  gathered on the banks of the sacramento  river but whom fremont and his men  attacked preemptively killing hundreds  of individuals  what was their reasoning for this  preemptive attack they had been told by  some colonists in the northern  sacramento river valley that this group  of california indian people might pose a  threat and so they attempted through  what i call in the book pedagogic  killing to  teach a lesson to other california  indians never to resist white people and  so kit carson and john c fremont and  their band of troopers killed as many as  800 california indian people on a single  morning thus in some ways setting the  stage for what would become an american  genocide now professor madley 1846 is  only three years before the 1849 gold  rush is does gold play a role gold plays  a major role in this book in fact if we  can look at the cover of the book the  editors insisted that there be gold leaf  on it in order to not so subtly hint at  the crucial role that gold played so  before the gold rush began there were  only 13 or 14 000 non-indian people  living here in california  but between 1846 and 1870 that  population surged to hundreds and  hundreds of thousands almost 400 000  people and so that massive demographic  switch suddenly made it possible for  newcomers to entirely change their  relationship to california indians where  before the gold rush california indian  people were central to the mexican  economy serving as ranch hands as farm  workers making wine lassoing cattle  once this surge of white people came  from europe and from the eastern united  states they were no longer so crucial to  the economy and many of the newcomers  saw them as competitors  in their search for the rapid  acquisition of what wealth that shiny  gold stuff and so the massacres  accelerated dramatically during the gold  rush period particularly against  california indian communities who are  engaged in gold mining from your book  during 1849 more than 65 000 immigrants  with an e often heavily armed  experienced with paramilitary  organizations and full of fear and  hatred towards indians arrived in  california yes so they often came  already organized with officers they had  sometimes corporations  but they also had military organizations  and the literature that they were  reading the print media was telling them  that it was very dangerous to cross the  vast interior of the united states that  they were at great danger from american  indian people so one group even hauled a  cannon all the way across the united  states from west virginia to california  and they didn't find any reason to use  it and they ended up being able to sell  it for only a single dollar in  sacramento because nobody really saw  california indians as a substantial  threat many california indians as i said  before were part of the economy they  were working in the mining industries  but because everybody came so heavily  armed it made it easy for them to kill  california indians the way this worked  was that these massacres were done at  distance so long-range weapons like  rifles were used that were at a much  longer range than california indian bows  and arrows had that allowed the  attackers to kill indian people without  putting themselves in harm's way why did  you focus on california  i'm from california i grew up in indian  country in far northern california my  father was a school psychologist so i  knew a lot of kuruk and shasta people  growing up and then we moved here to los  angeles  and suddenly i was wearing an indian on  my letterman's jacket but i was  wondering where are all of the indian  people it wasn't apparent to me at that  point that we are in right now on tongva  gabrielino land the most populous indian  city in the western united states i  didn't know that so i began to wonder  where everybody went and i began to  study this history and the further i  went into it  the more terrified i became by what i  was seeing that it was the state of  california our very first democratically  elected governor in california under u.s  rule declared and i quote that a war of  extermination will be waged until the  indian race becomes extinct and at first  i thought he was some kind of rogue  madman and then i see  right away  the state legislature puts the power of  the purse behind his words raises money  originally to the tune of half a million  dollars and later they raised over 1.9  million dollars to send out 24 state  militia campaigns against california  indians which kill large numbers of  people but which inspire an even larger  number of vigilante killings meanwhile  as i got further into the story i  realized that the us army was donating  all of the weapons and ammunition to the  militias  that the federal government later  reimburses the state of california over  nine hundred thousand dollars to pay for  militias and then that the united states  army is also involved and they actually  killed more people than the california  militiamen killed and in total the  numbers are quite staggering we know  that there's this great population  decline but this book actually documents  in almost 200 pages of appendices all of  the killings and what we've learned is  that an absolute minimum these  vigilantes militiamen and united states  army soldiers killed at an absolute  minimum  9400 california indian people perhaps as  many as 16 000 and probably  thousands of others  whose deaths were not recorded in the  ledgers and letters and journals and  newspapers and official reports of the  day that i combed over for years in  order to bring all of this material  together now it might seem macabre or  like some kind of antiquarian fetish but  what i wanted to do here  was present in some way a kind of  memorial to all the fallen california  indian people who are victims of this  state-sponsored mass murder  there was a day when i was doing my  research i was a graduate student at  yale i bumped literally into this  marble plinth there's a rotund at yale  with all the names of yale men and women  who have fallen in all of the wars that  have happened since eo was founded in  1701 and it was an aha moment i thought  we need something like this for  california indian people we need to  remember now we may not get a memorial  designed by someone like maya lin  but we do have these appendices  and  if you think about your own experience  of loss the rupture and pain when you've  lost a loved one in your life  and you look at these numbers you think  about the magnitude of what this means  for california indian communities it's  not just the political leaders were lost  it's the mothers the fathers the aunties  the uncles the grandparents the people  who knew how to make a bow perfectly  the people who knew how to  weave a beautiful basket with the  traditional designs of that community  and yet after all of this  this story of almost unimaginable horror  and the state's startling involvement  in navigating and creating these  programs  you have this dark cloud of genocide in  california's history but at the same  time you have this unbelievable story of  resilience and survival so today  there are 150 000 california indian  people 109 federally recognized tribes  up and down the state another 78  california communities who are not  recognized by the federal government so  all of these people  are the descendants of folks who somehow  figured out how to survive against all  but impossible odds how to resist and  live and save themselves and their  children in a world that wanted to  destroy them  with federal soldiers  looking for them militiamen searching  for them bounty hunters who were paid by  the human scalp or by the human head  they somehow evaded all of these forces  and survived so while it's a very dark  story in many ways it's also a story of  the triumph of the human will the  triumph of our human instinct for  survival against these odds that just  seem impossible  you use the term indians and not native  americans  yes well  in the academic world we often talk  about native americans or american  indians  but i grew up in what's called indian  country and most indian people that i  know on rancherias and reservations as  well as in urban areas refer to  themselves as simply indian now we know  that this is based on columbus's great  mistake he thought that he had reached  the east indies when in fact he'd only  reach the caribbean basin  but  this term now has a kind of political  and cultural resonance that is very  important so the single biggest  periodical that circulates around indian  country is called indian country today  that is if you will the national journal  or the national periodical of american  indian people throughout this country  but it is important to acknowledge that  it is a mistake but when we think of  indian country i'm glad you raised this  it's important to remember that we're an  indian country right now we're in tongva  gabriellino land to be specific but  anywhere you go in california you'll be  in someone's ancient ancestral homeland  lands that indian people have inhabited  for thousands of years or as many  believe since time immemorial and that's  true wherever you go in the united  states in fact wherever you go in this  hemisphere from the straits of magellan  to the shores of the arctic ocean you're  in indian country and one of the things  that happened because of this great loss  of life was the erasure  of indian people in the public  consciousness  not only have all the indian names for  the land forms and the rivers and the  lakes and inlets been erased but they've  been written over often with the very  names of the people who did the killing  so we have  carson peak  and numerous places named after john c  fremont two men who we alluded to  earlier  who were direct genocide perpetrators we  also have many schools and institutions  named after  leaders in high political places who  played roles in this genocidal process  so for example  hastings law school in san francisco  this state's oldest law school is named  after the first supreme court chief  justice a man who ruled in a case that  released eight men who others had  arrested for mass murder in the napa and  sonoma valleys also a man who personally  bankrolled the death squad that later  became the eel river rangers  one of these california state militia  units that was responsible for killing  hundreds of indian people over 280 by  their own official report perhaps 400 or  more according to journalists and  eyewitness accounts  so  the very history of this genocide is  woven into our state's place names  so the way we see the cartography of  california represents this not in terms  of letting people know that here a  massacre happened here a great  enslavement of california indian people  happened here on this reservation  hundreds of people starved to death but  rather in the celebration of the very  people who planned paid for and carried  out the killing and this is one of the  things i hope that this book might help  change and i should say that on  wednesday i had the opportunity to speak  with the governor of the state of  california and to present this book  in the capitol to him and to his cabinet  and to an assembly of california indian  leaders  and he  is going to acknowledge  that this was what he calls an actual  genocide so it's a rare moment  for an historian to actually have some  impact on public discourse and what i  hope can ultimately come out of this is  a wider dialogue about how we teach our  youth i hope that  this history can ultimately get part of  the state educational standards for what  young people in california learn you  know right now it's relegated california  indian history to the fourth grade and  most of us coming up have made the sugar  cube missions maybe we did them out of  tongue depressors but that's the only  time where we talk about california  history today  and my hope as an historian as an  activist historian is to put california  indians not only into the educational  standards but into our public discourse  and  perhaps we can have monuments that  commemorate these massacres that  commemorate the victims  and that tell the truth about what  happened and once we have a more  comprehensive understanding of this mass  violence the systematic state-sponsored  mass violence that took place in  california then we can begin a healing  process that might involve not only  memorials but state mandated days of  remembrance and a real discussion about  what the state of california and what  the government of the united states owes  to california indian people in light of  this horrific criminal past  what's an activist historian  well i see myself as an activist  historian in that i'm constantly  thinking about how the documents that  i'm uncovering and the way that i tell  the narrative echoes into our lived  experience and the ways in which  uncovering hidden pasts can change  public policy public discourse and the  way that we understand the past in order  to shape our future  you spoke of the first governor of  california  your book begins in 1846 california came  into the union in 1850  back then that was a pretty quick time  frame wasn't it or not it's it's an  extraordinary thing and i'm not a  subscriber to the idea of california  exceptionalism as you well know the book  pushes against that pretty hard  but it is a very rapid turnaround why  it's because of that extraordinary  wealth provided by the gold rush and the  extraordinary migration so the migration  to california is in fact the single  largest mass migration of the united  states 19th century  so by 1850 there's already a very  substantial number of people there but  it's also because the generals in charge  were not activists and they in fact  encouraged  this democratic process to happen very  quickly so california's state  constitutional convention happens in the  fall of 1849 almost a year before  statehood so they seize the day here in  california to move very rapidly and one  of the really interesting things about  that constitutional convention is that  they came  one vote away from enfranchising  california indians so we see again and  again in this story how the history of  california could have gone in a very  very different direction but for  a very few people  turning the tide in the direction of  what ultimately became  disenfranchisement and genocide your  book was recently recognized by the la  times as well what was the uh  recognition so this book just won the  los angeles times 2017 book award for  history which is  a very exciting accolade  for a book that uncovers a dark and very  uncomfortable paths to receive why do  you why do you end it in 1873 i ended it  in 1873 because that's when the last  major organized military campaigns  against california indians concluded the  book ends with the  1872-1873 modoc war in which california  indians modoc people hold off the  combined forces of the united states  army the california state militia and  the oregon state militia for months just  a handful of them but when they finally  surrender  the process is still not over the united  states army hangs and decapitates the  four surrendered leaders before shipping  their severed heads to the u.s army war  college in washington dc to the medical  museum there  so the process comes to a conclusion in  terms of that being the last major  campaign against california indians but  it doesn't mean that the genocide  absolutely stops there benjamin madley  is an associate professor of history at  ucla and the author of this book  an american genocide the u.s and the  california indian catastrophe 1846-1873  thanks for joining us on book tv thank  you very much

